WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.589
So, we talked about merging but there's
another way that you can introduce your

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.700
changes into a branch. That's with 'git
rebase'. Now, I do want to have a

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.410
disclaimer here: when you use 'git rebase'
you are changing history, but that might

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.470
be your preference and might be what
your team chooses to do.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.850
Let's talk about the differences. Say we
have two branches working here, this is the

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:27.119
'git merge' model and there's A B C and D
on one branch and E F on a separate

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:32.820
branch. Once we merge this branch into
the other branch, the way that's going to

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:39.750
appear in history is A B E C F D. So, it's
when the changes were made it -

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.280
it's accurate - but you might want your
history to be reflective of a different

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.180
nature you might want your history to
show when those changes were introduced

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.370
and make it more linear. That's when you
would use 'git rebase'. With rebase in the

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:59.879
same situation instead of merge it will
show the commits A B C D E F and their

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:03.870
logical order like I said it's really a
matter of preference and what your team

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.860
chooses to do. So, just be sure to check
with your collaborators and make sure

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.470
you know which one you're expected to be working with.

